  in these cases these are not  straightforward they're highly highly  complex matters involving uh detailed  issues of medicine and medical ethics  as well as the child's welfare  and i have confidence that our courts  and our judges will have looked at these  issues incredibly thoroughly incredibly  sensitively and have reached the the  right decision  and finally before i let you go let me  just quickly ask you about tonight's  debate on sky news what can we expect  from liz trust what does she have to do  listen i'm incredibly proud and excited  to be supporting liz to be the leader of  our party and the next prime minister  she's proven time and time again that  she's experienced that she delivers and  that she's got a bold and bright vision  for our united kingdom  she is  performing incredibly well i was just  speaking to party members yesterday  evening  about liz's candidature the response is  very very positive  people are coming to liz in their droves  um not just mps but also um memphis  debate what will we see from that very i  think we're going to see typical liz  we're going to see bright bold uh  exciting liz who can who is going to set  out a uh a bold vision for our country  supporting families and businesses with  the cost of living cutting taxes taking  a firm stance uh on brexit promises and  delivering for the country okay so ella  braverman thanks very much indeed for  coming in we appreciate it  now then as we were discussing there the  bank of england is set to make what's  forecast to be the biggest hike in  interest rates for 27 years in the face  of soaring inflation hundreds of  thousands of people could see their  mortgages and loan repayments jump by  hundreds of pounds  so let's get more on this and get the  thoughts of the personal finance expert  gemma godfrey who joins us now so gemma  godfrey first of all explain to us  why the bank of england might make this  move  well the bank of england has a really um  tough challenge where they're trying to  control inflation but they don't want to  tip us into recession so what we've been  hearing for the past few months is that  our economy has been slowing down we're  not spending as much companies aren't um  aren't selling as much and that  obviously risks jobs but obviously  everybody is aware that our everyday  living costs are rising they're rising  by five times um they're set to rise by  more than five times as much as the the  target that the bank of um england sets  and it's a war on all fronts it's food  it's energy you know people are really  really struggling so the bank of england  will try and increase rates to try and  slow this down um but obviously there's  there's calls that they've been too slow  um they're being or they're being too  aggressive and it's a very very tough  balancing act for for many millions of  families around the uk  well yeah so how will people feel the  impact if this rate rise does go ahead  will they see prices falling quite  quickly if it happens  no the the problem is that there's a lag  so the the bank of england will raise  interest rates fine and this is going to  be the sixth interest rate rise in a row  to put it in context rates were at a  historical low before that so it is  actually expected that the bank of  england could continue to raise interest  rates but the effect that we see is more  of an immediate um hardship on people  that are borrowing so um borrowing rates  will be going up so obviously variable  mortgages will be hit immediately um and  you know be harder for first-time buyers  and it'll be harder for people that you  know are borrowing money on credit cards  for example but in terms of the positive  impact so the impact in terms of um for  savers to get a better interest rate on  their savings or for prices to fall that  takes a lot longer and that's why it's  really really tricky for the bank of  england to make this move because in the  very very short term what it does the  immediate effect is it just makes  borrowing more expensive at a time when  it's more expensive to feed your family  to keep them warm to transport and to  travel to your job  so any tips for how people should manage  this if they're looking at interest  rights  interest rates going up what should they  do with their money  i mean it is one of the toughest times i  usually like to come on the show and  come and say here's three or four  different you know very easy tips and i  mean the first thing to say is that many  families are really really struggling  and so the first big tip is to speak up  is that there are you know energy  suppliers banks you know that really the  earlier you can speak to them and agree  um a payment plan the better because  what you want to do is avoid penalties  and most of all you want to avoid going  more into debt because debt is going to  be getting more and more expensive as  interest rates go up so  there is actually something that the  regulator has said that energy providers  need to do which is to provide an  affordable payment plan um and a lot of  energy providers have hardship funds  free support services as well um there's  retailers are offering discounts if you  want to donate um clothes that you don't  use anymore there's websites to help  check to see whether you're okay buying  the cheapest petrol though don't travel  too far to to go to those places um  yellow ticket items at um supermarkets  can also help you buy things like okay  we're out of time but brilliant advice  gemma i'm sorry to cut in but we must  say goodbye thanks so much for all your  tips
 

